<PageTitle>About — Blazor Atoms (WebAssembly)</PageTitle>

<h1>About this demo — standalone WebAssembly</h1>

<h2>The three samples</h2>
<p>
    Every playground in this repository is written once, in <code>Demos.Shared</code>, and hosted by
    three sibling apps — one per Blazor hosting model. Running the same components three ways is the
    point: it is how a component that quietly depends on one model gets caught.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppSvrDemo</strong> — Blazor Server, everything over a SignalR circuit.</li>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppAutoDemo</strong> — Blazor Auto, server-interactive first, WebAssembly once cached.</li>
    <li><strong>BlazorWebAppWasmDemo</strong> — standalone WebAssembly. <em>You are here.</em></li>
</ul>

<h2>What this one demonstrates</h2>
<p>
    This app is a genuinely <strong>standalone</strong> Blazor WebAssembly project: one project, no
    server host, no <code>.Client</code> split. Because there is no server to negotiate with, there
    are no render modes at all — you will not find a single <code>@@rendermode</code> in this sample,
    and adding one would break it. It boots from its own <code>wwwroot/index.html</code>, and
    <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.DevServer</code> is what serves it during
    development.
</p>
<p>
    This is the sample that proves the libraries need no ASP.NET Core host at all. Everything runs in
    the browser, so JavaScript interop is in-process rather than a round-trip, and the whole app
    publishes to plain static files — the deployment shape for static hosting such as GitHub Pages, a
    CDN, or blob storage. A <a href="RouteInfo">route dump</a> lists every <code>@@page</code>
    template discovered by reflection, which doubles as a check that reflection-based route discovery
    survives in the WebAssembly runtime.
</p>

